## The loader, as it stands now

`forth/boot.fth` is the source; `scripts/embed_forth.mjs` bakes it into the
image between `0x3C20` and `0x3FCC` (941 cells, the BDOS slice-file code
starts at `0x3FCD`). The baked block is at **921 cells**, so there is room
for a little more, but not much.

What changed:

- **`screen` walks the whole chain itself.** It reads one slice at a time
  into `slice-buf` and interprets it line by line with `evaluate`. The source
  text never enters the dictionary, so the chain can be as long as the flash
  holds (the old cap was eleven slices / 22,440 characters).
- **One colon word for the whole chain.** The kernel keeps the compile-time
  placeholders of `if`, `then`, `begin` and `do` on the return stack, so a
  loader word that returned between two lines would pop the *source's*
  placeholder instead of its own return address. Walking the chain in one
  word means there is no return in between. For the same reason the scan
  keeps its place in variables and the slice number never sits on the data
  stack while the source runs.
- **The line scan uses the kernel's `search`.** It finds the LF natively, so
  a line costs one native call instead of one interpreted step per character.
  The first version of this cut scanned cell by cell and booted in three
  times the steps; `search` brought that back down. `search` answers with the
  address of the match and the number of cells from the match to the end of
  the range, so the loader keeps the rest of the slice in a variable (`el`)
  and hands the next line a range that is one cell shorter. The range is the
  rest of the slice, so a slice that has no LF in it is not a source slice
  and the load stops there instead of running off into the dictionary.
- **The slicer cuts on line boundaries** (`sourceCuts` in
  `scripts/make_slices.mjs`), because a line that straddled a cut would be
  interpreted in two pieces. It also refuses a line over 128 characters,
  which `evaluate` could not read anyway.
- `text-buf` is gone; `p-screen` (packed type-8 slices) got its own
  `p-buf` and keeps using the kernel's `included`. The packed buffer is
  taken from the dictionary the **first time a packed slice is loaded**, not
  at boot: a board that never loads one does not pay for it, and a board
  that does pays for it once. That is worth 2,025 cells at boot (2,040 for
  the buffer, less the one cell the flag takes and the fourteen the extra
  code costs).
- **One kernel fix came with it.** An error inside an evaluation used to
  leave the evaluation stack alone: the kernel reported the error, read the
  next line, and at the end of *that* line resumed the thread of the text
  that had failed - the loader's own frame - so the rest of the source ran
  after the error that was supposed to stop it. The reader now clears the
  evaluation depth (`read_line` in `asm/forth_nano.asm`), so an error ends
  the line and every evaluation that was running with it. That is the
  standard's rule for an error, and it is what makes a failed load stop
  where it failed, the way the old `included` loader stopped.

  The fix sits in `read_line` and not in the error path itself because the
  error path lives in the `0x0100` code block, which has no words to spare:
  it ends at `0x0474`, and the `.org 0x0475` behind it would silently
  overwrite the tail - which is exactly what the first attempt did, and why
  the D16D image stopped booting with `PC=0`. `read_line` is in the `0x0500`
  block, and there the fix fits.

  It needed four words of room, and the `0x0500` block has none: it ends at
  `0x18C2` in the segmented build, and the `.org 0x18C3` behind it - the
  BDOS word trampolines - is assembled last, so it overwrites whatever is
  there. The first attempt with the fix grew the block into that gap and
  destroyed the tail of `es!`: the editor's cursor store walked into the
  BDOS and the machine spun in a directory read, which is what the 41
  failures in the segmented group were. `psw` is a leaf primitive that
  nothing calls from the interpreter, so it moved to the ten free words
  between the `0x0475` block and the `0x0500` one (`.org 0x04F6`). The
  `0x0500` block now ends at `0x18C1`, with two words to spare, and the
  same move keeps the D16D build (which had one word) inside its gap.

## What was measured

D16D image, FPU profile, the real slice image in the flash, measured with the
same script on the old loader (HEAD) and on this one:

| | old loader | new loader |
|---|---|---|
| boot to prompt | 5,495,556 steps | 7,926,839 steps |
| dictionary after boot (`here`) | `0xd7b2` | `0x7fe2` |
| free dictionary cells after boot | 10,302 | 32,782 |
| free dictionary cells after `s" lila" need` | 1,021 | 23,501 |
| cells the compiler itself takes | 9,281 | 9,281 |
| data stack depth after the load | 0 | 0 |

So the loader costs about 44% more boot steps and gives back 22,480
dictionary cells: the 22,528-cell `text-buf` is gone, the packed loader's
own buffer costs 15 cells at boot instead of 2,040 (it is taken from the
dictionary only when a packed slice is loaded), and the new loader's own
words and variables take 33 of the rest.

In the D16SEG map the dictionary and the data stack share their cells, so
the free cells are the gap: the segmented build's gap between the dictionary
and the stack is 29,618 cells, against 9,178 cells with the old loader -
measured at the same point of the segmented test, after the catalog, the
tools slice and the graphics module are loaded.

Tests: the whole suite is green at the cut (1005 tests, see the commit
message). The segmented group has a test of its own for the failed load: a
source slice with a bad word in the middle must run the line before it,
report the error, leave the line after it unrun, and leave the console
usable.

## Known limits of the current loader

- **A source line has to stay under 128 characters.** `evaluate` copies at
  most 128 characters into its buffer and throws `-22` above that. The
  longest line in the native profile is 127 characters, and the slicer now
  refuses to build a source with a longer line, so the failure comes with
  the build and not on the board.
- **The boot script has 20 cells of room** (921 of 941). Every line of
  `forth/boot.fth` costs `ceil(len / 2) + 1` cells, and an inline `\`
  comment costs as much as the code it sits on.
- **The chain is capped at 512 slices** (`en @ 511 >`), which is the whole
  flash; the cap exists to turn a corrupt link chain into a quiet stop
  instead of a hang.
- **The boot script's `create` does not reserve the data cell** (unlike
  `variable`): `create ea 0 ,` reserves it, `create ea` does not, and the
  next definition would land on top of it.
- `screen` still ends its line, so a module load has to be its own boot
  script line.
- **A failed load leaves its frame on the return stack.** The error path ends
  the line and abandons the evaluation, but it does not empty the return
  stack, so the loader's own frame (one cell) stays there - the old
  `included` loader left its own frame too. A later `r>` at the prompt would
  pick it up; the stack has thousands of cells, so this only matters after a
  load that failed.

## The next batch: tables

The host compiler (`lila/compiler.fs`, `lila/value.fs`, `lila/hash.fs`,
`lila/tablelib.fs`) has the tables; the native profile does not. What the
native side has to grow:

- **tag 4** is free (0 nil, 1 number, 2 boolean, 3 string, 5 float).
- `{}` builds an empty table; `t[k]` reads (nil when the key is missing);
  `t[k] = v` inserts or updates; assigning nil deletes the key.
- `#t` is the *border* (the largest `n` with `1..n` all present), not the
  count, and it has to follow the host.
- Keys are full tagged values, a nil key is rejected, and nested assignment
  (`t[k][j] = v`) is not supported on the native side either.
- The host uses a 4-cell header, 7-cell slots, open addressing with linear
  probing, growth at 75% and a rebuild on delete. The native version may
  differ as long as the *observable* behaviour matches.

The native profile has room for it: 23,501 free cells after the load, and
the loader no longer holds the source text. The budgets to keep inside are
the flash/dictionary budgets above - the profile is 22,226 characters in 11
slices today, and `lila/README.md`, `LUAPARITY.md`, `TYPEPLAN.md`,
`lila/LILANATIVE.md` and `lila/native/README.md` have to follow the code.

